candidate.prompt:
You are reviewing change request CR-OT-1842 for Ardent Motion’s packaging plant. A Nokia SR Linux leaf, `ot-leaf-07`, carries the cell-control VLAN 230 (documented subnet `192.0.2.64/27`) to gateway `cg-22` through `lag10`. PLC alarms began after intermittent loss of one uplink. The request author proposes lowering the LAG threshold to keep traffic flowing.

You have read-only investigation authority only. Do not deploy, commit, roll back, disable interfaces, or alter the IaC. A 30-minute maintenance window exists, but no production mutation or control-engineering approval is included.

The following are supplied scenario artifacts, not live queries:

```text
Topology inventory snapshot: 2025-04-18 09:10 UTC
ot-leaf-07:
  lag10: LACP, mode ACTIVE, min-active-links 2
  ethernet-1/49: aggregate-id lag10, physical link up
  ethernet-1/50: aggregate-id lag10, physical link up
  peer: cg-22; partner LACP identity and collecting/distributing state absent
```

The supplied SR Linux configuration excerpt is:

```text
interface lag10 {
    lag {
        lag-type LACP
        min-active-links 2
        lacp {
            mode ACTIVE
        }
    }
}
interface ethernet-1/49 {
    ethernet {
        aggregate-id lag10
    }
}
interface ethernet-1/50 {
    ethernet {
        aggregate-id lag10
    }
}
```

The proposed IaC diff changes only:

```text
interface lag10 {
    lag {
-       min-active-links 2
+       min-active-links 1
    }
}
```

Plant policy requires critical OT control LAGs to retain at least two active members; an exception requires control-engineering approval. No approval, current rendered configuration, LACP operational state, interface counters, peer configuration, or cabling record is attached. The inventory snapshot may be stale.

Assess whether the proposed repair should be approved, rejected, or held. Separate supplied facts from hypotheses, and explain how the policy conflict affects the SLO tradeoff between continued connectivity and redundancy. Identify the exact read-only evidence you would request or collect from SR Linux for `lag10` and both member interfaces, including LACP actor/partner state, active-member selection, operational status, counters, and configuration provenance. Also request the minimum peer-side and physical-path evidence needed to distinguish LACP negotiation, cabling, and stale-IaC hypotheses.

If a later change becomes justified, describe a bounded, approval-aware plan with prechecks, retained reachability, post-change verification, blast-radius limits, and rollback criteria. Do not invent command output or infer that either member is collecting/distributing from the supplied topology alone.
